<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch06_P03_Multiple_of_both</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>(a) Give an example of a whole number that is a multiple of both {@aa@} and {@bb@}, but is not a multiple of \({@aa@} \times {@bb@} = {@prodab@}\).</p>
<div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) The example above ends: the product is a multiple of 2 and a multiple of 3, and therefore a multiple of 6. Part (a) shows that the same step fails for {@aa@} and {@bb@}. Which fact about 2 and 3 makes the step valid?</p>
<div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>A number that is a multiple of \(a\) and of \(b\) is a multiple of their least common multiple. {@aa@} and {@bb@} share the factor {@gcdab@}, so their least common multiple is {@lcmab@}, not {@prodab@}: {@lcmab@} itself is a multiple of both {@aa@} and {@bb@}, but not of {@prodab@}. So <q>a multiple of \(a\) and of \(b\) is a multiple of \(ab\)</q> is not true in general.</p>
<p>When two numbers have no common factor other than 1, their least common multiple is their product. 2 and 3 have no common factor other than 1, so a multiple of 2 and of 3 is a multiple of \(2 \times 3 = 6\). That is the reason the last step of the example is valid. The other three facts are true, but each is just as true for {@aa@} and {@bb@}, where the step fails, so none of them can be the reason.</p>
<p>The same reasoning with 8 and 3 gives 24, which the last exercise in this chapter needs.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1000000</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026062900</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables><text><![CDATA[/* Pairs with a common factor, so a common multiple need not be a multiple of the product. */
pairL: [[4, 6], [6, 9], [4, 10], [6, 8], [6, 10], [10, 15]];
pairpick: rand(pairL);
aa: pairpick[1];
bb: pairpick[2];
gcdab: gcd(aa, bb);
lcmab: aa*bb/gcdab;
prodab: aa*bb;
s_assert(is(gcdab > 1), true);
s_assert(is(mod(aa, bb) # 0), true);
/* Facts about 2 and 3. Each foil is true, but also holds for aa and bb. The correct one last. */
optsC: [
 [oprod, false, "\\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\)"],
 [omult, false, "6 is a multiple of both 2 and 3"],
 [oeven, false, "6 is even"],
 [ocop, true, "2 and 3 have no common factor other than 1"]
];
]]></text></questionvariables>
